# [PF-DROP] (D135) the size-cap ladder's LAST rung: drop the VM hybrid's
# prefilter. `(\p{Xwd})` under `-e utf8` is a VM hybrid (the capture) whose
# byte-DFA prefilter alone put the artifact over the 1,000,000-byte total
# cap (1,026,588 B); before D135 it was REFUSED at default settings, and the
# rung dropped the prefilter so it shipped at ~31 KB, answering identically
# (a prefilter is a filter).
#
# [START-LANDING] (lane slbuild, 2026-10-10): `(\p{Xwd})` now takes the
# `landing` start, which drops its REVERSE machine, so it fits with its
# prefilter (359,103 B) and the rung no longer fires for it -- a FIT mover
# (docs/design/start_landing.md §7). Its block stays: it now holds the
# landing artifact's spans. The SECOND block, `(\p{Xwd})x`, takes no landing
# (one character from the start does not decide the match), still drops its
# prefilter at the default (1,040,601 B hybrid > the cap), and is the rung's
# witness here (S420's harness target). tests/uprops/run_uprops_tests.sh §5 checks the
# stamps, the note, `--size-cap=refuse`'s refusal and the whole-code-point
# membership; this file checks spans and the capture.
#
# Every expectation was verified against libpcre2 10.46 (the reference,
# pcre2test over ssh, 2026-09-30) and 10.48 (this Mac): `\p{Xwd}` is
# PCRE2's UCP `\w`, so a connector punctuation (U+203F) and a combining mark
# (U+0301) ARE members, and ASCII punctuation is not.

pattern (\p{Xwd})
encoding utf8
m "a" 0 1
g 1 0 1
m "_" 0 1
g 1 0 1
m " \xc3\xa9" 1 3
g 1 1 3
n "!?"
m "\xe2\x80\x94x" 3 4
g 1 3 4
m "\xd9\xa3" 0 2
g 1 0 2
m "\xe2\x80\xbf" 0 3
g 1 0 3
m "\xe6\x9d\xb1" 0 3
g 1 0 3
m "\xcc\x81" 0 2
g 1 0 2
n ""
n " \t-.,;"

# The rung's witness since [START-LANDING]: `(\p{Xwd})x` is a VM hybrid
# whose prefilter alone is over the cap and which takes the reverse pass, so
# the rung drops the prefilter at default settings. Every expectation is
# libpcre2 10.46's (PCRE2_UTF, this Linux box, 2026-10-10).
pattern (\p{Xwd})x
encoding utf8
m "ax" 0 2
g 1 0 1
m "_x" 0 2
g 1 0 1
m " \xc3\xa9x" 1 4
g 1 1 3
n "!?x"
m "\xe2\x80\x94xx" 3 5
g 1 3 4
m "\xd9\xa3x" 0 3
g 1 0 2
m "\xe2\x80\xbfx" 0 4
g 1 0 3
m "\xe6\x9d\xb1x" 0 4
g 1 0 3
m "\xcc\x81x" 0 3
g 1 0 2
n "a"
n ""
